In this lesson, we'll look at right pyramids and right cones, and you'll see there's a really satisfying pattern connecting them to prisms and cylinders. A right pyramid is just a pyramid where the pointy top sits directly above the middle of the base, and if that base is a circle instead of a polygon, you've got a right cone. For both shapes, the volume is exactly one-third of the matching prism or cylinder that wraps around them. So think of it this way: same base, same height, but you only get a third of the space filled in. Next up, surface area. For a pyramid, you take the area of the base and add half the perimeter of the base times the slant height, where the slant height is the length down the middle of each triangular face. For a cone, it's the area of the circle plus pi times the radius times that same slant height. And here's a handy connection: the slant height, the vertical height, and the radius form a right triangle, so the slant height equals the square root of the height squared plus the radius squared. Oh, and keep your units matching, so centimetres give you square centimetres and cubic centimetres. Now let's see it in action.